 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What's going on",
   "text": "MPS IIIA happens because a gene called SGSH does not make a working enzyme, which is like a tiny tool that helps the body do a job [E7b907fc, E9276c06]. This enzyme's normal job is to help break down a long sugar chain in the body called heparan sulfate [E0d3b201]. When the enzyme is missing, heparan sulfate cannot be broken down properly and builds up inside small parts of cells called lysosomes [Ec586fdd, Ed62fa17]. A paper reports that this buildup can also lead to extra glycogen, another kind of sugar, collecting in the liver [E6e128f9]. A paper reports that behavior changes and a decline in thinking skills may be a possible result of this buildup along with inflammation in the brain [E4af2811].",
   "citations": [
    "E7b907fc",
    "E9276c06",
    "E0d3b201",
    "Ec586fdd",
    "Ed62fa17",
    "E6e128f9",
    "E4af2811"
   ],
   "status": "ok"
  },
  {
   "key": "similar",
   "title": "Diseases that are biologically similar",
   "text": "MPS IIIB, MPS IIIC, and MPS IIID are biologically similar to MPS IIIA because each one is also caused by a missing lysosomal enzyme needed to break down heparan sulfate [E8a7d8c7, E108a4c3, Ef8f5811]. In all of these conditions, heparan sulfate builds up inside lysosomes, the same kind of storage problem seen in MPS IIIA [Eae4e3d8, E7373ae4, E9f300a9]. According to Reactome, the genes behind MPS IIIA, MPS IIIB, MPS IIIC, and MPS IIID all sit together on the same heparan sulfate breakdown pathway [E32d11c0, E7719c79, E977014a, Ef75bef1]. MPS I (Hurler) and MPS II (Hunter) are also biologically similar, since their genes sit on that same heparan sulfate breakdown pathway according to Reactome [Ee3a2899, Ecba7335]. According to HPO, MPS IIIA, MPS IIIB, and MPS IIIC can all show a feature called dense calvaria, a change in the skull bones [E92597bb, E8ddb732, E4bf3fd5].",
   "citations": [
    "E8a7d8c7",
    "E108a4c3",
    "Ef8f5811",
